  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1228 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nToday through Wednesday: Dry Today - Widespread Rain Early Week\n\nSubtle upper level ridging is in place for today which is aiding\nin leading to an area of clearing cloud cover over the CWA at \nthe time of this writing. The breaks in the clouds should allow \ntemperatures to warm up into the 60s area-wide today and some \nlocations may even approach the upper 60s to near 70s if enough \nheating can occur. Beyond today, plenty of cloud cover is \nforecast for much of this next week, so be sure to get out and \nenjoy the limited sunshine today! Dry conditions are forecast \nfor today through most of tonight.\n\nThe next weather system to bring widespread rainfall to the\nregion is currently moving east over the western CONUS. This \nfeature should be over or just east of the Rockies by sunrise \ntomorrow morning. A strong plume of mid level theta-e will be in\nplace out ahead of this system as it approaches the Upper \nMidwest. Widespread rainfall will be a result from the upper \nlevel lift combined with plentiful moisture throughout the \natmospheric column. The main downside is that the latest \nforecast guidance is trending towards lesser rainfall totals \nwith this system through Wednesday morning. We should know if \nthis trend will come to fruition by tomorrow morning, when the \nwidespread rainfall was originally expected to begin. There are \nstill models that suggest widespread rainfall begins to move \nover the region on Monday morning but the trend is towards more \nisolated to scattered rain showers through mid afternoon before \nthe first widespread rain bands move in. This is supported in a \nmajority of the hi-res guidance, including both the 01/12z HREF \nand REFS. After the initial widespread rain band moves through, \nit is likely that at least scattered rain showers will continue \ninto Monday night before another band moves in early Tuesday \nmorning.\n\nAdditional widespread rainfall will almost certainly continue on \nTuesday. The main axis of the H500 trough will remain to the \nwest of the forecast area which should keep the moisture axis \nover the Driftless Region. Dependent upon how the surface low \ntracks going into Wednesday, rain shower chances may continue \ninto the day if wrap around moisture can move in over the CWA. \nWidespread rainfall totals of a half inch or more are still\nhigh (+70%) based on the NBM, HREF, REFS, and LREF output. \nHowever, the potential for 1 inch of rain or more has been \ndecreasing, most notably in the 01/00z to 01/12z LREF, which \ndecreases that potential by upwards of 20 percent in the newest \ngrand ensemble run. In addition to the rain, temperatures will \nremain well above average in the upper 50s to mid 60s on Monday \nand Tuesday before beginning to cool down on Wednesday as cooler\nair wraps around the upper level low. In fact, the record warm \nlow temperature for the month of November at KRST and KLSE may \nbe tied or broken on Tuesday morning if the forecast lows end up\npanning out.\n\nLate This Week: Trending Drier with Above Normal Temperatures\n\nThe trough that provided the rain earlier in the week should weaken \nand lift northward late this week. Westerly to northwesterly flow is \nforecast initially behind the system before another potential trough \ndigs south out of the prairie provinces of Canada next weekend. This \nsynoptic pattern favors reinforced cooler air over the Upper Midwest.\nEven so, temperatures may still be slightly above average for highs\nand lows during this time frame. Dry conditions are forecast for\nFriday into Saturday, but there could be some chances of precipitation\non Saturday depending upon moisture quality and the track of \nthe next trough.",
